concepts.body:
Behaviour lives in an `impl` block. The first parameter decides how a method gets the value:

```rust
impl Ship {
    fn new(name: &str) -> Self {            // associated function: no self
        Ship { name: name.to_string(), guns: 0 }
    }
    fn describe(&self) -> String {          // borrows: read only
        format!("{} ({} guns)", self.name, self.guns)
    }
    fn arm(&mut self, n: u32) {             // borrows mutably: may change fields
        self.guns += n;
    }
    fn scuttle(self) -> String {            // takes ownership: ship is gone afterwards
        self.name
    }
}

let mut ship = Ship::new("Pearl");          // call with Type::
ship.arm(4);                                 // call with value.
```

Pick the weakest receiver that works: `&self` to read, `&mut self` to change, `self` only when the method really consumes the value. `Self` is shorthand for the type of the `impl`.

When you only care about one pattern, a full `match` is noisy:

```rust
if let Cargo::Map(place) = &cargo {
    println!("a map to {place}");
}
```

`let ... else` is the opposite: it binds the pattern for the rest of the block, or runs the `else` block, which must leave (`return`, `break`, `continue` or panic):

```rust
fn barrels(word: &str) -> Option<u32> {
    let Ok(n) = word.parse::<u32>() else {
        return None;
    };
    Some(n * 2)
}
```

This keeps the happy path flat instead of nesting it inside `if let`.

A binding in a pattern **moves** the matched data by default. If you only *borrow* the value, you can't move a `String` out of it:

```rust
fn map_len(ship: &Ship) -> usize {
    match ship.hold {
        Cargo::Map(place) => place.len(), // error[E0507]: cannot move out of `ship.hold`
        _ => 0,
    }
}
```

Match on a **reference** instead. Then every binding becomes a reference too (this is called *match ergonomics*):

```rust
match &ship.hold {
    Cargo::Map(place) => place.len(), // place: &String
    _ => 0,
}
```

The older spelling is `Cargo::Map(ref place)`, which asks for a reference in just that one binding. Copy data such as `u32` is never a problem: binding it copies it. When you **own** the value, for example inside a method that takes `self`, moving out of it is fine.

